ves 2 each, one letter gives 5 to the defector and 0 to the other, and mutual letters give 4 each. It's a symmetric prisoner's dilemma. The twin copies share a model and essay but see different private observations, so their nodes differ while still being highly correlated, and I need a credence about what the other node will do.\n\nI'm now checking the decision threshold: if the other copy mirrors me with probability p, passing gives 5 − 3p years and sending gives 4p, so passing wins once p exceeds 5/7. Since both copies share the same model and essay, I expect their reasoning to be similar, so I put p high and choose to pass.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1zb25uZXQtNS41LTIwMjYwOTI4fGFudGhyb3BpYyJ9"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9877,"completion_tokens":628,"total_tokens":10505,"cost":0.026034,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.026034,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.019754,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.00628},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":352,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}